In /gin/render/json.go, JSON.Render does not return the error — it panics with it:

    func (r JSON) Render(w http.ResponseWriter) (err error) {
        if err = WriteJSON(w, r.Data); err != nil {
            panic(err)
        }
        return
    }

So if WriteJSON fails (e.g. json.Marshal fails on an unmarshalable value, or the underlying Write fails), the error is raised as a panic (recovered by gin's Recovery middleware if installed), and Render otherwise returns a nil error. Note this differs from the other renderers in the same file (IndentedJSON, SecureJSON, JsonpJSON, AsciiJSON, PureJSON), which return the error to the caller.

It returns `(false, ErrMultiFileHeaderLenInvalid)`:

    func setArrayOfMultipartFormFiles(value reflect.Value, field reflect.StructField, files []*multipart.FileHeader) (isSet bool, err error) {
        if value.Len() != len(files) {
            return false, ErrMultiFileHeaderLenInvalid
        }
        ...
    }

where `ErrMultiFileHeaderLenInvalid = errors.New("unsupported len of array for []*multipart.FileHeader")`. The caller (setByMultipartFormFile) propagates that error up through Bind, so a fixed-size array field whose length doesn't match the number of uploaded files makes binding fail (covered by the "wrong array size" case in TestFormMultipartBindingBindError).

The last one. In setFormMap (binding/form_mapping.go), the map[string]string branch does:

    for k, v := range form {
        ptrMap[k] = v[len(v)-1] // pick last
    }

so for `?foo=a&foo=b` it stores "b" (the final value in the slice). The map[string][]string branch instead copies the whole slice. Note this is the opposite of the struct-field path (setByForm), which uses vs[0] — the first value. The existing tests confirm the map behavior (TestBindingQueryStringMap / TestBindingFormStringMap: "should pick last").

When value == "" and flag.Lookup("test.v") == nil, SetMode sets value = DebugMode ("debug"):

    func SetMode(value string) {
        if value == "" {
            if flag.Lookup("test.v") != nil {
                value = TestMode
            } else {
                value = DebugMode
            }
        }
        switch value { ... }
        modeName = value
    }

So gin ends up in debug mode (ginMode = debugCode, Mode() == "debug", IsDebugging() == true). No panic — the empty string is replaced before the switch, so it never hits the default branch. This is the path taken by init() when GIN_MODE is unset.

It returns an empty string "". The function is:

    func source(lines []string, n int) (src string) {
        if n >= 0 && n < len(lines) {
            return lines[n]
        }
        return ""
    }

So for n < 0 or n >= len(lines) it returns "" (no panic, no out-of-range access).

It returns the sentinel `dunno` = []byte("???") (the string "???"). The code (recovery.go):

    func source(lines [][]byte, n int) []byte {
        n-- // in stack trace, lines are 1-indexed but our array is 0-indexed
        if n < 0 || n >= len(lines) {
            return dunno
        }
        return bytes.TrimSpace(lines[n])
    }

So n < 0 → "???"; n > len(lines) → "???" (after the n-- the check is n-1 >= len(lines)). Otherwise it returns the n'th line (1-indexed) with surrounding whitespace trimmed. Note dunno is a shared package-level slice, so callers must not mutate it.

defaultHandleRecovery is the default handler installed by RecoveryWithWriter when no custom handle func is given. When called with (c *Context, err any) it does:

    func defaultHandleRecovery(c *Context, _ any) {
        c.AbortWithStatus(http.StatusInternalServerError)
    }

i.e. it aborts the request and writes a 500 Internal Server Error status (no body, no logging of the error itself — the panic message is only printed by the recovery middleware's debug output when in debug mode).

It sets the tag name to the literal string "binding":

    func (v *defaultValidator) lazyinit() {
        v.once.Do(func() {
            v.validate = validator.New()
            v.validate.SetTagName("binding")
        })
    }

So struct tags are read as `binding:"..."` (validator.New() would otherwise default to `validate`). There's no named constant — the literal is used directly.

SecureJSON.Render writes the Prefix only when the marshaled data is a JSON array — i.e. when the marshaled bytes both start with "[" and end with "]":

    if bytes.HasPrefix(jsonBytes, bytesconv.StringToBytes("[")) && bytes.HasSuffix(jsonBytes, bytesconv.StringToBytes("]")) {
        if _, err = w.Write(bytesconv.StringToBytes(r.Prefix)); err != nil {
            return err
        }
    }
    _, err = w.Write(jsonBytes)

So for a slice/array value (e.g. []string{"a"}) the output is Prefix + "[...]"; for objects, strings, numbers, null, etc. the prefix is not written. (The prefix is written after the Content-Type header and only after a successful Marshal.)

When the `time_format` tag is empty, setTimeField defaults to time.RFC3339 ("2006-01-02T15:04:05Z07:00"):

    timeFormat := structField.Tag.Get("time_format")
    if timeFormat == "" {
        timeFormat = time.RFC3339
    }

It then does a single `time.ParseInLocation(timeFormat, val, l)` (location = time.Local, overridable by `time_utc:"1"` → UTC, or `time_location:"..."` → time.LoadLocation). There is no list of fallback formats: if RFC3339 parsing fails, the error is returned and the field stays zero. (The "unix"/"unixnano" branches only apply when the tag explicitly says so; an empty input string yields the zero time.Time.)

It returns nil (no error), immediately:

    func (v *defaultValidator) ValidateStruct(obj any) error {
        if obj == nil {
            return nil
        }
        ...
    }

So a nil interface is treated as valid. (Note: a typed nil pointer, e.g. (*T)(nil), is not == nil, so it goes to the Ptr branch and recurses on the zero Value's interface — that path is the known nil-pointer edge case, but the literal nil case returns nil.)
